4. Dormant Provenance and Project-Credit Covenant

No obligation under this Section exists until the particular Licensee actually receives a valid Provenance Notice from the Licensor.

A Provenance Notice may require one or more of the following with respect to Covered Material identified in the notice:

(a) Provenance Acknowledgement. The Licensee must state, in the wording specified by the Licensor or in substantially equivalent factual wording, that the identified Covered Material or implementation derives from algorithmic research, software, documentation, or other Covered Material distributed by MatrixFreeSama. Such acknowledgement concerns provenance only and need not characterize the Licensor as the legal author of code not legally authored by the Licensor.

(b) Direct Provenance Answer. If the Licensor directly asks the Licensee whether the identified Covered Material or implementation was derived from the Covered Material, the Licensee must answer truthfully and must not knowingly deny the identified derivation.

(c) Designated Project Credit. The Licensee must add, replace, or display the Designated Project Credit specified by the Licensor in public-facing project credit, repository presentation, documentation, website presentation, application about-box or equivalent project-level presentation identified in the notice.

(d) Removal or Replacement of Licensee Project Credit. The Licensee must remove or replace a public-facing project-level credit for the Licensee or another distributor where the Provenance Notice expressly requires that removal or replacement. This does not require falsification or deletion of legally required records, immutable third-party archives outside the Licensee's control, or factual contributor records where applicable law requires their preservation.

A Designated Project Credit may be a fictional identity or an identity with no research or implementation participation. Its display must not be represented as proof of legal authorship, ownership, contribution, or endorsement.

Unless the Provenance Notice grants a longer period, the Licensee has thirty (30) days after actual receipt to bring public-facing materials under the Licensee's control into compliance. Future public distributions made after that period must comply with the notice.

A Provenance Notice may apply only to that Licensee and the Covered Material identified in the notice. It does not impose obligations on an Independent Implementation or on another person who has not received a valid Provenance Notice.

